{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>论利未说</b>——即「他说关于利未的话」；这是拉实（Rashi on Deuteronomy 33:8）的用语，众注释家亦持此意见。类似于אִמְרִי לִי אָחִי הוּא（创世记20:13，「论到我说，他是我的哥哥」）、וַיִּשְׁאֲלוּ אַנְשֵׁי הַמָּקוֹם לְאִשְׁתּוֹ（创世记26:7，「那地方的人问起他的妻子」），以及许多类似的用法。依我之见，既然摩西在祝福中提到了流便的名字和犹大的名字——יְחִי רְאוּבֵן（「愿流便存活」）、שִׁמְעָה ה' קוֹל יְהוּדָה（创世记49:8「雅威啊，求你听犹大的声音」）——却不提其余支派的名字来祝福他们，经文便记着וּלְלֵוִי אָמַר（「论利未说」）、לְבִנְיָמִין אָמַר（「论便雅悯说」），以此表明众支派站在他面前，他便呼叫利未，注目于他，说：תֻּמֶּיךָ וְאוּרֶיךָ（「你的完全和你的乌陵」），同样地，每一个支派他都面对面地祝福。" "<b>Whom You tried at Massah</b> — whom You tested with a trial. And the reason is that he was a devout man (חָסִיד) in everything in which You tried him, and no fault was found in him, except for the waters of Meribah, for which You already punished him, because concerning the Calf he acted for the honor of HaShem. This is the language of Ibn Ezra (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 33:8). And in the Sifrei (Sifrei Beshalach §Shamet): \"אֲשֶׁר נִסִּיתוֹ בְּמַסָּה — You tried him many times and he was found whole in all the trials. תְּרִיבֵהוּ עַל מֵי מְרִיבָה — סְקוּפִּים נִסְקַפְתָּ לוֹ עַל מֵי מְרִיבָה (charges were framed against him concerning the waters of Meribah); if Moses said (Numbers 20:10) שִׁמְעוּ נָא הַמּוֹרִים ('Hear now, you rebels') — what did Aaron and Miriam do?\" And what seems correct in my eyes according to the plain sense is that <b>בְּמַסָּה</b>, in its plain meaning, is the place where they tested HaShem at Rephidim (Exodus 17:7). He says: 'Your תֻּמִּים and Your אוּרִים shall be forever for Your devout man and for his seed after him, whom You tried at Massah' — when the children of Israel sinned and tested HaShem at the waters, he was not among the congregation that assembled there against HaShem, for he believed in HaShem and in His word, that He would bring forth water for them from the flinty rock. And the second time: תְּרִיבֵהוּ — You contended with him and punished him over the waters of Meribah of Israel, for because of their strife the punishment fell upon him, yet he did not act treacherously against HaShem, for even at first he believed that אַתָּה הַהֹפְכִי הַצּוּר אֲגַם מָיִם ('You who turn the rock into a pool of water'), all the more so this time, when he had already seen such a thing. Moses hints that that sin was on account of the people, for because of their strife Moses and Aaron thought they needed to do what they did, lest the Shekhinah withdraw on account of their sin. And this is what is said (Psalms 106:32): וַיַּקְצִיפוּ עַל מֵי מְרִיבָה וַיֵּרַע לְמֹשֶׁה בַּעֲבוּרָם ('And they provoked wrath at the waters of Meribah, and it went ill with Moses on their account'). And the meaning of <b>תְּרִיבֵהוּ</b> is like רִיבָה ה' אֶת יְרִיבַי (Psalms 35:1, 'Contend, O YHWH, with those who contend with me') — that You rendered him the judgment of the quarrel that the children of Israel waged against HaShem. And it is possible that its meaning is as stated in the Sifrei, that they are סְקוּפִּים, that is, accusations, hinting at the smallness of the sin and the greatness of the punishment, in the manner of בִּקְרֹבַי אֶקָּדֵשׁ (Leviticus 10:3, 'through those near Me I will be sanctified'). And so is the opinion of Onkelos (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 33:8), who said: דְּנַסִּתוֹהִי בְּנִסֵּיתָא וְהֲוָה שְׁלִים, מֵרִיב בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וּמִנַּסּוֹתָם אֶת ה' ('You tried him in a trial and he was whole; from the strife of Israel and their testing of HaShem'). Since he was whole the first time, all the more so the second time, for he believed in the word of HaShem, and You examined his heart at the waters of Meribah and found his heart faithful before You — for it is revealed and known before the Throne of Your Glory that he believes in You and in Your words, but on account of Israel the punishment fell upon him, as I explained in the verse גַּם בִּי הִתְאַנַּף ה' בִּגְלַלְכֶם (Deuteronomy 1:37, 'HaShem was also wrathful with me on your account'). Now, at first he spoke concerning the priests, for he said of Levi that the Urim and Tumim would belong to His devout man among them, and afterward concerning the entire tribe — that You tried them with the Calf and in Egypt and in the wilderness, and therefore they shall teach His ordinances to Jacob. And so is the opinion of Onkelos, who said: כָּשְׁרִין אִלֵּין דְּיַלְּפוּן דִּינָךְ ('these are worthy to teach Your laws'), and so forth. Or he speaks concerning the future, as the words of Ibn Ezra: וְיֹרוּ מִשְׁפָּטֶיךָ — concerning all of them, וְיָשִׂימוּ קְטוֹרָה — concerning the priests among them."], :book "deuteronomy", :voice :ramban, :lang :zh-Hans, :chapter 33, :timestamp "2026-07-28T01:52:56.649646619Z", :translated-from :en, :model "glm-5.2"}